#include <iostream>

int main() {
    long long x = 0;
    int k = 0;
    std::cin >> x >> k;
    const long long mask = 1LL << k;
    std::cout << "bit " << k << ": " << ((x >> k) & 1) << '\n';
    std::cout << "set: " << (x | mask) << '\n';
    std::cout << "cleared: " << (x & ~mask) << '\n';
    std::cout << "toggled: " << (x ^ mask) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
With n items, a subset is one yes or no choice per item, which is exactly n bits. So the integers from 0 to `(1 << n) - 1` list every subset once, and bit i of `mask` says whether item i is in it:

```cpp
for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); ++mask) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        if ((mask >> i) & 1) {
            // item i is in this subset
        }
    }
}
```

For items a, b, c, mask 5 is `101`, so it picks a and c. `__builtin_popcount(mask)` is a g++ built-in that counts the 1 bits of an `unsigned int`, which is the size of the subset. `std::popcount` is C++20 and not available here, though `std::bitset::count` gives the same number. For a `long long` mask use `__builtin_popcountll`.

Nothing is stored per subset: each one is just an int on the stack, decoded on the fly. The cost is O(2^n * n) work, which is instant for n up to about 20 and far too slow past about 30. With an `int` mask, `1 << n` only works up to n of 30.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::vector<std::string> items = {"a", "b", "c"};
    const int n = static_cast<int>(items.size());
    for (int mask = 0; mask < (1 << n); ++mask) {
        std::cout << '{';
        bool first = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
            if ((mask >> i) & 1) {
                if (!first) {
                    std::cout << ", ";
                }
                std::cout << items[i];
                first = false;
            }
        }
        std::cout << "} size " << __builtin_popcount(mask) << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

